WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 46: Bleeding Kansas and John Brown's Raid on Pottawatomie

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.960
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:15.200
[Rebecca] Seven years before the first cannon fired at Fort Sumter, the American Civil War began in the rolling prairie grass of the Kansas Territory.

4
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:28.160
[Rebecca] In 1854, the passage of the Kansas-Nebraska Act repealed the Missouri Compromise, declaring that whether Kansas entered the Union as a free or slave state would be decided by popular sovereignty.

5
00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.640
[Rebecca] The vote of the settlers themselves.

6
00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:35.360
[Rebecca] Almost overnight, Kansas became a violent magnet for fanaticism.

7
00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:54.480
[Rebecca] Abolitionist societies in Boston raised funds to ship hundreds of settlers armed with Sharps rifles shipped in crates labeled Bibles, while thousands of heavily armed pro-slavery vigilantes from Missouri known as border ruffians crossed the river to hijack elections and terrorize freesoil towns.

8
00:00:54.480 --> 00:01:06.160
[Rebecca] In May 1856, the violence culminated in a midnight massacre along Potawatomi Creek, led by an uncompromising Old Testament-style zealot named John Brown.

9
00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.960
[Rebecca] Here is George with the story.

10
00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:29.360
[George] The Act organized the two vast territories of Kansas and Nebraska, but it contained a fateful political bargain.

11
00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:37.920
[George] It repealed the historic Missouri Compromise Line of 1820, which had banned slavery north of 36 degrees 30 minutes latitude.

12
00:01:37.920 --> 00:01:48.400
[George] Instead, Douglas introduced the doctrine of popular sovereignty, allowing the white male settlers of each territory to decide for themselves whether to legalize slavery.

13
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:54.880
[George] Douglas naively believed this democratic mechanism would remove the slavery question from national politics.

14
00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:59.920
[George] Instead, it transformed the Kansas Prairie into a ferocious, zero-sum civil war.

15
00:02:00.680 --> 00:02:12.600
[George] Northern abolitionists and southern slaveholders both understood that whichever side controlled Kansas would control two seats in the United States Senate and permanently tilt the balance of national power.

16
00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:29.320
[George] In Boston, wealthy abolitionists formed the New England Emigrant Aid Company, raising hundreds of thousands of dollars to transport thousands of free-soil pioneer families to Kansas, establishing fortified anti-slavery towns like Lawrence, Topeka, and Manhattan.

17
00:02:29.320 --> 00:02:41.400
[George] Senator Stephen Douglas' popular sovereignty doctrine set the prairie ablaze, transforming Kansas into a bloody ideological battleground between pro-slavery Missourians and abolitionist pioneers.

18
00:02:41.400 --> 00:02:46.840
[George] Across the Missouri River, pro-slavery forces reacted with apoplectic fury.

19
00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:58.440
[George] Missouri Senator David Rice Acheson mobilized a paramilitary army of Missouri frontiersmen known to history as border ruffians, armed with shotguns, bowie knives and whiskey jugs.

20
00:02:58.440 --> 00:03:17.640
[George] The border ruffians rode across the border on election days, terrorizing election judges, stuffing ballot boxes with thousands of fraudulent votes, and electing a fraudulent pro-slavery territorial legislature that enacted draconian laws making criticism of slavery a felony punishable by death.

21
00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:28.040
[George] In the North, prominent abolitionist minister Henry Ward Beecher declared that a Sharps breech-loading rifle was a more persuasive moral argument against slavery than a Bible.

22
00:03:28.040 --> 00:03:40.440
[George] New England societies promptly shipped thousands of state-of-the-art sharps military rifles to Kansas free staters, concealing the weapons inside wooden shipping crates labeled Bibles and Carpentry Tools.

23
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:42.920
[George] Both sides formed armed militias.

24
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:58.000
[George] And throughout 1855 and early 1856, rival factions ambushed one another along prairie roads, burned homesteads, and turned the territory into an armed camp known in headlines across the globe as Bleeding Kansas.

25
00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:09.920
[George] Henry Ward Beecher congregation shipped wooden crates labeled Bibles that actually contained state-of-the-art sharps repeating rifles, giving rise to the famous frontier term Beecher's Bibles.

26
00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:15.120
[George] The smoldering ideological powder keg exploded in May 1856.

27
00:04:15.120 --> 00:04:32.080
[George] A corrupt federal grand jury in Lecompton, composed entirely of pro-slavery partisans, indicted the leaders of the Free State town of Lawrence for treason, declaring the town's two abolitionist newspapers and its fortified Free State Hotel to be illegal public nuisances.

28
00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:45.760
[George] On the morning of May 21, 1856, Douglas County Sheriff Samuel Jones led an army of 800 heavily armed border ruffians flying crimson flags that read Southern Rights into the streets of Lawrence.

29
00:04:45.760 --> 00:04:51.280
[George] The town's leaders, seeking to avoid a bloodbath, ordered their citizens not to resist.

30
00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:54.560
[George] The pro-slavery mob went on a destructive rampage.

31
00:04:54.560 --> 00:05:04.080
[George] They dragged the printing presses of the Herald of Freedom and the Kansas Free State into the streets, smashed them with sledgehammers, and threw the type into the river.

32
00:05:04.080 --> 00:05:15.120
[George] They bombarded the Stone Free State Hotel with cannon fire before setting it ablaze with torches, and they looted and burned private homes and shops, leaving Lawrence a smoking ruin.

33
00:05:15.120 --> 00:05:26.720
[George] The pro-slavery posse ransacked the Free State Hotel with artillery and threw the printing presses of the Herald of Freedom into the Kansas River, inflaming abolitionist fury across the North.

34
00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:35.160
[George] Just one day after the sack of Lawrence, an equally sensational act of violence occurred on the floor of the United States Senate in Washington.

35
00:05:35.160 --> 00:05:48.840
[George] South Carolina Representative Preston Brooks walked into the Senate chamber and beat Massachusetts Senator Charles Sumner unconscious with a heavy gold-headed Gutta Perchicane following Sumner's speech denouncing the crime against Kansas.

36
00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:59.880
[George] News of both outrages reached an isolated, impoverished homestead along Oswatomi Creek, where 56-year-old John Brown was living with four of his adult sons.

37
00:05:59.880 --> 00:06:13.320
[George] Brown was an intense, devoutly religious Calvinist from Connecticut with gaunt cheeks, piercing grey eyes, and an unshakable conviction that he was a chosen instrument of God's divine wrath against the sin of human slavery.

38
00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:19.720
[George] Brown had suffered financial failures and poverty his entire life, but he possessed an iron will.

39
00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:26.280
[George] Hearing of the sacking of Lawrence and the beating of Sumner, Brown flew into a cold, terrifying fury.

40
00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:33.320
[George] He looked at his sons and declared, Something must be done to show these barbarians that we, too, have rights.

41
00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.240
[George] Without the shedding of blood, there is no remission of sin.

42
00:06:37.240 --> 00:06:48.760
[George] John Brown was an old testament figure of grim, unyielding conviction, believing he was a divine instrument chosen by God to break the jaws of the wicked and overthrow human bondage by sword.

43
00:06:48.760 --> 00:07:01.160
[George] On the night of Saturday, May 24, 1856, John Brown led a hand-picked strike squad consisting of four of his sons, a son-in-law, and two neighbors toward Potawatomi Creek.

44
00:07:01.160 --> 00:07:10.600
[George] The men were armed with revolvers, hunting rifles, and razor-sharp two-edged artillery broadswords that Brown had obtained from an Ohio Masonic Lodge.

45
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:22.320
[George] Traveling under cover of pitch-black darkness, Brown's squad targeted three isolated cabins inhabited by prominent pro-slavery settlers associated with the violent border ruffian courts.

46
00:07:22.320 --> 00:07:28.800
[George] Around 10 o'clock, they arrived at the cabin of James Doyle, an active pro-slavery partisan.

47
00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:32.240
[George] Posing as travelers, Brown knocked on the wooden door.

48
00:07:32.240 --> 00:07:38.480
[George] When Doyle opened it, Brown's men dragged Doyle and his two adult sons outside into the darkness.

49
00:07:38.480 --> 00:07:42.880
[George] Ignoring the frantic screams of Doyle's wife, Brown gave the signal.

50
00:07:42.880 --> 00:07:52.880
[George] His sons hacked the three men to death with broadswords, splitting their skulls and severing their hands before Brown himself fired a single bullet into Doyle's forehead.

51
00:07:52.880 --> 00:08:06.800
[George] Brown's squad then marched to two nearby cabins, dragging out Alan Wilkinson, a pro-slavery postmaster and legislator, and William Sherman, a violent brawler, executing both men in the same brutal fashion.

52
00:08:06.800 --> 00:08:15.040
[George] By dawn, five pro-slavery men lay dead along the creek, their mutilated bodies left in the grass as a terrifying warning.

53
00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:27.680
[George] Under cover of darkness along Potawatomi Creek, Brown and his sons dragged five pro-slavery settlers from their log cabins, executing them with broadswords in a terrifying act of ideological warfare.

54
00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:40.080
[George] The Potewatomi massacre sent shockwaves of sheer terror through pro-slavery ranks and instantly transformed bleeding Kansas from sporadic political skirmishing into savage, merciless guerrilla warfare.

55
00:08:40.080 --> 00:08:47.520
[George] Enraged border ruffians mobilized under Captain Henry Clay Pate, burning the Free State settlement of Oswatomi.

56
00:08:47.520 --> 00:08:54.160
[George] John Brown, now a wanted fugitive with a bounty on his head, embraced his role as a guerrilla commander.

57
00:08:54.160 --> 00:09:11.560
[George] On June 2nd, at the Battle of Black Jack, Brown led 30 free Staters in a fierce four-hour firefight against Pate's superior force, forcing Pate and 32 border ruffians to surrender, a clash regarded by many historians as the first true armed battle of the Civil War.

58
00:09:11.560 --> 00:09:22.920
[George] In August, at the Battle of Osuwatomi, Brown with just 40 men held off an assault by 400 pro-slavery milishicmen, earning the legendary moniker Oswatomi Brown.

59
00:09:22.920 --> 00:09:27.240
[George] Over 200 settlers lost their lives during 1856.

60
00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:33.240
[George] And millions of dollars in property was destroyed before newly appointed territorial governor John W.

61
00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:36.840
[George] Geary used federal troops to impose a fragile truce.

62
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:48.600
[George] Guerrilla skirmishes raged across eastern Kansas throughout the bloody summer of 1856, turning farmsteads into armed garrisons and prefiguring the national conflagration of the Civil War.

63
00:09:48.600 --> 00:09:54.280
[George] Bleeding Kansas ultimately ended in a decisive political victory for the Free State cause.

64
00:09:54.280 --> 00:10:01.960
[George] Outnumbered by the relentless influx of northern homesteaders, pro-slavery forces could not maintain their fraudulent control.

65
00:10:01.960 --> 00:10:17.800
[George] In 1859, Kansas voters overwhelmingly ratified the antislavery Wyandotte Constitution, and on January 29, 1861, as southern states began seceding, Kansas was admitted to the Union as a proud free state.

66
00:10:17.800 --> 00:10:26.280
[George] Meanwhile, John Brown's baptism of blood in Kansas convinced him that slavery could only be destroyed through armed slave insurrection.

67
00:10:26.280 --> 00:10:41.080
[George] Three years after Potawatomi, in October 1859, Brown led his famous raid on the Federal Armory at Harper's Ferry, Virginia, an event that polarized the nation beyond repair and marched America straight into the Civil War.

68
00:10:41.080 --> 00:11:00.160
[George] John Brown's ghost still haunts the Kansas Prairie, captured in John Stewart Curry's colossal mural inside the Kansas State Capitol, depicting a towering, wild-eyed prophet holding a Bible and a rifle, reminding us that the ideals of American freedom were forged in the crucible of blood and fire.

69
00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:08.160
[Rebecca] Bleeding Kansas and the raid on Potawatomi reveal the terrifying extremes of conviction that shattered the American Republic.

70
00:11:08.160 --> 00:11:15.040
[Rebecca] It shows that when compromise fails and injustice festers, the storm of history will eventually sweep all before it.

